---
description: Prefer single-line calls and expressions; avoid breaking arguments across lines.
globs: **/*.java
alwaysApply: false
---

# Prefer Single-Line Formatting

Keep most statements on one line. Do not break method or constructor arguments onto separate lines unless the line would be unreasonably long.

## Constructor Declarations

Keep the full parameter list on the same line as the constructor name. Do not put each parameter on its own line.

```java
// ❌ BAD
FoundingLocationSearchTask(
        FoundingLocationFinder finder,
        Resident resident,
        SettlementStatPreference preference,
        int radiusBlocks,
        Consumer<FoundingLocationResult> onSuccess,
        Consumer<String> onFailure) {

// ✅ GOOD
FoundingLocationSearchTask(FoundingLocationFinder finder, Resident resident, SettlementStatPreference preference, int radiusBlocks, Consumer<FoundingLocationResult> onSuccess, Consumer<String> onFailure) {
```

Same rule for method declarations: `void foo(Type a, Type b) {`, not one parameter per line.

## Constructor and Method Calls

```java
// ❌ BAD
return new PolicySelectionContext(
        civ.hasTechnology(Tech.ANCIENT_ERA),
        civ.getPolicies().size(),
        civ.getCapitolTown().getCultureLevel(),
        hasOracleBuff,
        civ.getBonusPolicies().size()
);

// ✅ GOOD
return new PolicySelectionContext(civ.hasTechnology(Tech.ANCIENT_ERA), civ.getPolicies().size(), civ.getCapitolTown().getCultureLevel(), hasOracleBuff, civ.getBonusPolicies().size());
```

```java
// ❌ BAD
someObject.doSomething(
        arg1,
        arg2,
        arg3
);

// ✅ GOOD
someObject.doSomething(arg1, arg2, arg3);
```

## Chained Calls

Prefer one line when readable:

```java
// ❌ BAD
return civ.getCapitolTown()
        .getCultureLevel();

// ✅ GOOD
return civ.getCapitolTown().getCultureLevel();
```

## When Multi-Line Is Acceptable

Use multiple lines only when a single line would be excessively long or harm readability (e.g. many string literals, complex lambdas, or large inline blocks). Default to single-line for typical calls with a handful of arguments.

When editing existing code, collapse unnecessary multi-line argument lists you touch into single lines.
